 of Federated Learning (FL). Recently, Federated Distillation (FD) has emerged as a novel FL paradigm characterized by distinct communication dynamics. FD utilizes ensemble distillation, facilitating the exchange of model outputs—typically soft probabilities—instead of traditional aggregations of loss functions or update coefficients. This approach allows for the transmission of highly compressed information, potentially reducing the frequency of communications. Unlike standard FL methods where data is sent to the server, FD transfers distilled models, termed "ghost networks," to the clients. The FD framework remains valid regardless of the underlying base network architecture, as the communication structure decoupled from the primary network size, enabling scalable FL. FD offers several distinct advantages over conventional FL strategies. This paper provides a comprehensive theoretical analysis of FD's unique communication properties. We demonstrate that FD supports the exchange of compressed model parameters, distinct from the loss functions or update coefficients used in traditional FL, while maintaining convergence guarantees and accuracy comparable to standard FL methods. These theoretical findings are supported by experimental evidence, with FD implemented using the FedDistortPytorch library. FL experiments were conducted on the MNIST dataset using the {pytorch}\footnote{\url{https://pytorch.org/}} deep learning framework, employing custom FedDistortPytorch implementations for federative distillation. Our results indicate that FD achieves model convergence with 5 to 10 times fewer communications than traditional FL approaches. Furthermore, FD enables the training of deep models alongside much simpler associated ghost networks on resource-constrained mobile devices. This work validates that FD serves as a promising and effective alternative to classical FL schemes. The associated code for FL and Federated Distillation is accessible at \url{https://github.com/AliMehdip/FedDistortPytorch}.

