\section{INTRODUCTION}

Smartphone image sharing has become a daily habit for many people. However, an analysis of image sharing platforms reveals a significant rise in inadvertently shared private information. This trend has prompted platform providers to introduce privacy checkers—simple image classifiers designed to help users detect and prevent the accidental sharing of sensitive data. These checkers analyze image content to flag potentially private information, although they remain imperfect. Some images lack sufficient visible cues to allow for accurate privacy level determination. Others contain private items that are too small to be detected or classified by current object-level privacy analysis models. Finally, some images contain clear, non-private content that, despite its apparent safety at a image level, could lead to privacy breaches.

