\subsection*{Related Work}

\subsubsection*{Multi-Task Reinforcement Learning}
Multi-task RL (MT-RL) involves solving multiple tasks simultaneously, often leading to a common convergence goal while minimizing sample complexity for each. We categorize existing MT-RL solutions into two streams: multi-task training and multi-task inference. In multi-task training, agents are trained on various tasks either separately or jointly. Separate training involves training each agent independently, often resulting in sub-optimal final networks and wasted training samples. Joint training trains all agents together to achieve optimal networks but struggles with sample efficiency. Our local critic training offers a third alternative by training local critics in parallel with the main network to improve multi-task training efficiency. Joint inference combines solutions from both separate and joint training, selecting the most suitable network based on specific task requirements.

